    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hi, i'm looking at some data for a new roundabout design near a major junction in london. can you give me a quick comparison of how signalized intersections vs roundabouts typically handle traffic throughput and what the main differences are in terms of delay and flow? thanks.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In general, roundabouts tend to handle traffic throughput more efficiently than signalized intersections, with higher throughput capacity. This is because roundabouts focus all traffic onto the central lane, reducing lane-changing and merging delays, and allow continuous movement of traffic around the circle, minimizing stops and starts. However, signalized intersections are better suited for higher volumes and tend to have lower average delay due to the ability to control the flow of traffic in specific directions. Roundabouts, on the other hand, can be prone to congestion delays at rush hour or low volumes, but overall, they tend to provide a smoother flow.",
      "source": "target",
